<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert, King of Naples</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1276">1276</a>–<a href="/years/1343">1343</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Robert the Wise, Robert of Anjou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert the Wise was Angevin king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> from 1309 to 1343. The leading champion of the Guelph cause in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, he opposed the emperor Henry VII and failed to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> from its Aragonese kings. He made Naples a centre of culture, welcoming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Boccaccio</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a>. His granddaughter Joanna I succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-king-of-naples.jpg" alt="Robert, King of Naples" width="320" height="597" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RobertNeapol_Martini,_Simone.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
